.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/@eslint/plugin-kit Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "ESLint Plugin Kit"
.PP
.SH "Description"
.PP
A collection of utilities to help build ESLint plugins\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
For Node\&.js and compatible runtimes:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install @eslint/plugin\-kit
# or
yarn add @eslint/plugin\-kit
# or
pnpm install @eslint/plugin\-kit
# or
bun add @eslint/plugin\-kit
.fi
.PP

.PP
For Deno:

.PP
.PP
.nf
deno add @eslint/plugin\-kit
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
This package exports the following utilities:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB`ConfigCommentParser`\fP - used to parse ESLint configuration comments (i\&.e\&., \fR/* eslint-disable rule */\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB`VisitNodeStep` and `CallMethodStep`\fP - used to help implement \fRSourceCode#traverse()\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB`Directive`\fP - used to help implement \fRSourceCode#getDisableDirectives()\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB`TextSourceCodeBase`\fP - base class to help implement the \fRSourceCode\fP interface
.PP
.SS "\fRConfigCommentParser\fP"
To use the \fRConfigCommentParser\fP class, import it from the package and create a new instance, such as:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { ConfigCommentParser } from "@eslint/plugin\-kit";

// create a new instance
const commentParser = new ConfigCommentParser();

// pass in a comment string without the comment delimiters
const directive = commentParser\&.parseDirective(
    "eslint\-disable prefer\-const, semi \-\- I don't want to use these\&.",
);

// will be undefined when a directive can't be parsed
if (directive) {
    console\&.log(directive\&.label); // "eslint\-disable"
    console\&.log(directive\&.value); // "prefer\-const, semi"
    console\&.log(directive\&.justification); // "I don't want to use these\&."
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
There are different styles of directive values that you'll need to parse separately to get the correct format:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { ConfigCommentParser } from "@eslint/plugin\-kit";

// create a new instance
const commentParser = new ConfigCommentParser();

// list format
const list = commentParser\&.parseListConfig("prefer\-const, semi");
console\&.log(Object\&.entries(list)); // [["prefer\-const", true], ["semi", true]]

// string format
const strings = commentParser\&.parseStringConfig("foo:off, bar");
console\&.log(Object\&.entries(strings)); // [["foo", "off"], ["bar", null]]

// JSON\-like config format
const jsonLike = commentParser\&.parseJSONLikeConfig(
    "semi:[error, never], prefer\-const: warn",
);
console\&.log(Object\&.entries(jsonLike\&.config)); // [["semi", ["error", "never"]], ["prefer\-const", "warn"]]
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRVisitNodeStep\fP and \fRCallMethodStep\fP"
The \fRVisitNodeStep\fP and \fRCallMethodStep\fP classes represent steps in the traversal of source code\&. They implement the correct interfaces to return from the \fRSourceCode#traverse()\fP method\&.

.PP
The \fRVisitNodeStep\fP class is the more common of the two, where you are describing a visit to a particular node during the traversal\&. The constructor accepts three arguments:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtarget\fP - the node being visited\&. This is used to determine the method to call inside of a rule\&. For instance, if the node's type is \fRLiteral\fP then ESLint will call a method named \fRLiteral()\fP on the rule (if present)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRphase\fP - either 1 for enter or 2 for exit\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRargs\fP - an array of arguments to pass into the visitor method of a rule\&.
.PP

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { VisitNodeStep } from "@eslint/plugin\-kit";

class MySourceCode {
    traverse() {
        const steps = [];

        for (const { node, parent, phase } of iterator(this\&.ast)) {
            steps\&.push(
                new VisitNodeStep({
                    target: node,
                    phase: phase === "enter" ? 1 : 2,
                    args: [node, parent],
                }),
            );
        }

        return steps;
    }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
The \fRCallMethodStep\fP class is less common and is used to tell ESLint to call a specific method on the rule\&. The constructor accepts two arguments:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRtarget\fP - the name of the method to call, frequently beginning with \fR"on"\fP such as \fR"onCodePathStart"\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRargs\fP - an array of arguments to pass to the method\&.
.PP

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { VisitNodeStep, CallMethodStep } from "@eslint/plugin\-kit";

class MySourceCode {
    traverse() {
        const steps = [];

        for (const { node, parent, phase } of iterator(this\&.ast)) {
            steps\&.push(
                new VisitNodeStep({
                    target: node,
                    phase: phase === "enter" ? 1 : 2,
                    args: [node, parent],
                }),
            );

            // call a method indicating how many times we've been through the loop
            steps\&.push(
                new CallMethodStep({
                    target: "onIteration",
                    args: [steps\&.length]
                });
            )
        }

        return steps;
    }
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRDirective\fP"
The \fRDirective\fP class represents a disable directive in the source code and implements the \fRDirective\fP interface from \fR@eslint/core\fP\&. You can tell ESLint about disable directives using the \fRSourceCode#getDisableDirectives()\fP method, where part of the return value is an array of \fRDirective\fP objects\&. Here's an example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { Directive, ConfigCommentParser } from "@eslint/plugin\-kit";

class MySourceCode {
    getDisableDirectives() {
        const directives = [];
        const problems = [];
        const commentParser = new ConfigCommentParser();

        // read in the inline config nodes to check each one
        this\&.getInlineConfigNodes()\&.forEach(comment => {
            // Step 1: Parse the directive
            const { label, value, justification } =
                commentParser\&.parseDirective(comment\&.value);

            // Step 2: Extract the directive value and create the `Directive` object
            switch (label) {
                case "eslint\-disable":
                case "eslint\-enable":
                case "eslint\-disable\-next\-line":
                case "eslint\-disable\-line": {
                    const directiveType = label\&.slice("eslint\-"\&.length);

                    directives\&.push(
                        new Directive({
                            type: directiveType,
                            node: comment,
                            value,
                            justification,
                        }),
                    );
                }

                // ignore any comments that don't begin with known labels
            }
        });

        return {
            directives,
            problems,
        };
    }
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRTextSourceCodeBase\fP"
The \fRTextSourceCodeBase\fP class is intended to be a base class that has several of the common members found in \fRSourceCode\fP objects already implemented\&. Those members are:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRlines\fP - an array of text lines that is created automatically when the constructor is called\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetLoc(nodeOrToken)\fP - gets the location of a node or token\&. Works for nodes that have the ESLint-style \fRloc\fP property and nodes that have the Unist-style \fR\fRposition\fP property\fP\&. If you're using an AST with a different location format, you'll still need to implement this method yourself\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetLocFromIndex(index)\fP - Converts a source text index into a \fR{ line: number, column: number }\fP pair\&. (For this method to work, the root node should always cover the entire source code text, and the \fRgetLoc()\fP method needs to be implemented correctly\&.)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetIndexFromLoc(loc)\fP - Converts a \fR{ line: number, column: number }\fP pair into a source text index\&. (For this method to work, the root node should always cover the entire source code text, and the \fRgetLoc()\fP method needs to be implemented correctly\&.)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetRange(nodeOrToken)\fP - gets the range of a node or token within the source text\&. Works for nodes that have the ESLint-style \fRrange\fP property and nodes that have the Unist-style \fR\fRposition\fP property\fP\&. If you're using an AST with a different range format, you'll still need to implement this method yourself\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetText(node, beforeCount, afterCount)\fP - gets the source text for the given node that has range information attached\&. Optionally, can return additional characters before and after the given node\&. As long as \fRgetRange()\fP is properly implemented, this method will just work\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetAncestors(node)\fP - returns the ancestry of the node\&. In order for this to work, you must implement the \fRgetParent()\fP method yourself\&.
.PP

.PP
Here's an example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { TextSourceCodeBase } from "@eslint/plugin\-kit";

export class MySourceCode extends TextSourceCodeBase {
    #parents = new Map();

    constructor({ ast, text }) {
        super({ ast, text });
    }

    getParent(node) {
        return this\&.#parents\&.get(node);
    }

    traverse() {
        const steps = [];

        for (const { node, parent, phase } of iterator(this\&.ast)) {
            //save the parent information
            this\&.#parent\&.set(node, parent);

            steps\&.push(
                new VisitNodeStep({
                    target: node,
                    phase: phase === "enter" ? 1 : 2,
                    args: [node, parent],
                }),
            );
        }

        return steps;
    }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
In general, it's safe to collect the parent information during the \fRtraverse()\fP method as \fRgetParent()\fP and \fRgetAncestor()\fP will only be called from rules once the AST has been traversed at least once\&.
